<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Hardened runtime is required for notarization, and it turns off four
      things this app needs. Each key below is a hole punched in it, so each one
      is justified rather than copied.

      Deliberately ABSENT: com.apple.security.app-sandbox. cc-miner reads
      ~/.claude, a dot-directory the sandbox excludes — which is why the Mac App
      Store is not a distribution channel for this app at all, and why Developer
      ID + notarization is.
    -->

    <!--
      V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime. Without these two,
      Electron does not start at all.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      MEASURED, not assumed — this is the one that was in doubt.

      Library validation requires every loaded Mach-O to be signed by the same
      Team ID as the host process (or by Apple). cc-miner loads two kinds that
      are not:

        libduckdb.dylib   112 MB, shipped by DuckDB, signed by DuckDB
        quack/httpfs/aws  DuckDB extensions fetched at first query into
                          ~/.duckdb/extensions and dlopen'd from there

      With hardened runtime and WITHOUT this key, the sidecar dies before it can
      serve anything. See the experiment recorded in the P5 commit.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      The sidecar is spawned as a child of the app and inherits its environment;
      Electron sets ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE and friends on the way. Hardened
      runtime otherwise scrubs DYLD_* and related variables from children.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Outbound network: DuckDB fetches its own extensions on first query, S3 mode
      talks to AWS, and the updater polls GitHub Releases. No inbound server —
      quack_serve binds 127.0.0.1 only.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
